{
  "answer": "A Shadow Cabinet is the organized opposition to an actual cabinet. In a Westminster‑style democracy the leading opposition party appoints a “shadow minister” for each government minister’s portfolio. Together they form a Shadow Cabinet.\n\nThe function of a Shadow Cabinet is twofold:\nScrutiny – Shadow ministers monitor and critique the policies and actions of their counterparts. They attend parliamentary questions, submit written and oral responses, and press the government to explain its decisions.\nAlternatives – They develop and communicate their own policy proposals for each portfolio, thereby presenting a ready‑made alternative government. If the opposition wins an election, the Shadow Cabinet can immediately step into the official roles.\n\nThe system gives the opposition a structured way to hold power to account and to demonstrate that it is capable of governing. While the term originates in the UK, similar arrangements exist in other parliamentary systems (e.g., in Canada, Australia, and New Zealand) and informal “shadow” groups occasionally appear in other contexts (e.g., the U.S. “Shadow Cabinet” blogs of some think‑tanks).",
